{"duration_api_ms":245048,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"7bd454c6-25b4-4e0b-ade8-5f0b643a66fa","total_cost_usd":0.3593216,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10245,"cache_read_input_tokens":4538,"output_tokens":31743,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":23537},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10245,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":31743,"cache_read_input_tokens":4538,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10245,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10245},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":31743,"cacheReadInputTokens":4538,"cacheCreationInputTokens":10245,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.3593216,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":23537,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"```json\n{\n  \"merged_document\": \"# Rate limiting and the 429 contract for gateway clients\\n\\nEvery credential has a cap. When that cap is spent the gateway answers 429 at the edge — without waking the service behind it — so the refusal costs the platform almost nothing and costs a caller that files it under transport error a great deal. An earlier draft of this note argued for a client-side token bucket that mirrored the gateway's own counters; that section was left out on purpose, since two counters that disagree are worse than one counter that refuses, which is the whole reason the header exists.\\n\\nRequests are counted against the credential that presented them, over a fixed window of 60 seconds whose start the gateway doesn't disclose, and never against a connection. Opening a second socket therefore buys a caller nothing, since the count follows the credential wherever it goes, including across hosts. A client spread across eight worker processes is throttled at exactly the point one process would have been — and pays for the extra file descriptors as well.\\n\\nThe default allowance is 100 requests in a window — enough for every interactive use of this API the platform team has seen, since a caller that needs more is almost always doing batch work under an interactive credential — and a credential marked for batch work is allowed 1000 requests in the same window. A refusal is not an outage, whatever the error class in a client library happens to call it, and it isn't a bug in the gateway either — it is capacity being held for a request somebody else has already been promised, and waiting is the only correct answer. Callers that retry at once are the largest single reason the cap is there at all.\\n\\n## How to read a refusal\\n\\nThe refusal carries a Retry-After header. Its value is a whole number of seconds to wait, and it is present on every refusal the gateway sends. Waiting that long and then continuing is the whole of the contract — a client that does it needs nothing else from this document. The gateway keeps no memory of who backed off politely, so waiting longer than the header asks earns a caller no credit at all.\\n\\nThe body of the refusal is JSON, and it names what the gateway calls the \\\"window\\\" it counted in. It names the cap and the tier as well. None of those fields is a substitute for the header, and a caller that parses the body to compute its own wait is doing the same arithmetic twice. The body is there so that a human reading a log afterwards can see why the request was refused — nothing in it is meant for the retry loop.\\n\\n## How to retry well\\n\\nFour rules, given in the order they should be applied.\\n\\n1. Honour the header, every time.\\n   The gateway has already done that arithmetic, with information the caller does not have, and it does the same on the 503 path, even though the cause of the refusal there is an entirely different one. A wait derived on the client side is a guess about a counter it cannot see. There is no case at all where a guess is the better of the two.\\n\\n2. Retry only what is safe to retry, which is a smaller set than the set of responses that aren't a success.\\n   A 200 wants nothing, a 429 wants the stated wait, a 500 may be retried once that wait has passed, and a 503 is the overload path. The four cases are not interchangeable. A client that folds every non-success into one branch will retry hardest during exactly the incident the cap was installed to survive. Avoid writing that loop.\\n\\n3. Batch is different.\\n   A batch credential is allowed 1000 requests in a window — fifty times the default allowance — measured over exactly the same window, not as a separate counting scheme. The tier is set on the credential when it is issued and cannot be asked for per call. The window, the header and the body are identical to the interactive case, so a client written for one tier needs no change at all to run against the other. Nothing else about the two tiers differs in any way.\\n\\n4. Log every refusal seen, and keep the log for at least a week.\\n   A caller that cannot say how often it was refused last week cannot argue for a larger cap, and nobody at the far end will assemble that argument on its behalf. The counts are the whole of the argument, and without them a request is only a preference. The log line should carry the credential, the window and the wait.\\n\\n## Other refusals and their codes\\n\\nNot all are caps. The gateway refuses a request for three reasons and only one of them is the one above; two of the three have nothing to do with how much traffic a caller has sent in the window it is currently in. A credential may be suspended, a route may be closed for maintenance, or a body may exceed the 5 megabyte limit and be refused before it has been read at all. None of those three clears itself by waiting for a stated number of seconds.\\n\\nThe distinction matters more to a retry loop than it does to a reader. A loop that waits and retries against a suspended credential spends its whole budget without ever reaching the service — and the log afterwards shows nothing at all except a long run of refusals, which reads like an outage and isn't one, with the wasted budget being the caller's own. Reading the status code rather than the class it belongs to is what separates the two cases, and it costs one comparison.\\n\\n## Asking for an increase\\n\\nA cap can be raised, and the number of caps raised without a measurement behind them is 0. Bring a week of refusal counts, the shape of the traffic across the day, and the deadline that traffic is serving. The platform team looks at whether the load is smooth or bursty before it looks at the number at all — a burst is cheaper to smooth than it is to serve at its peak. A caller that can move half its work to a quieter hour usually gets what it needs without any change to the cap at all, which is the outcome everyone prefers; it is the cheapest fix available to anybody, and it is available to almost everybody.\\n\\nRequests reach the platform team through the usual channel, and they are answered within two working days — chasing it doesn't make that take fewer. There is no expedited path and no exception list.\",\n  \"decisions\": [\n    {\n      \"slot\": \"title\",\n      \"candidates\": [\n        {\"text\": \"# Rate limiting and the 429 contract for gateway clients\", \"document\": \"source_a.md\"},\n        {\"text\": \"# What a client should do when the gateway says 429\", \"document\": \"source_b.md\"}\n      ],\n      \"chosen\": \"# Rate limiting and the 429 contract for gateway clients\",\n      \"reason\": \"Base document's title kept.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"slot\": \"heading: retry rules section\",\n      \"candidates\": [\n        {\"text\": \"## How to retry well\", \"document\": \"source_a.md\"},\n        {\"text\": \"## What the client does\", \"document\": \"source_b.md\"}\n      ],\n      \"chosen\": \"## How to retry well\",\n      \"reason\": \"Base heading kept for the rule-list section.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"slot\": \"heading: other refusals section\",\n      \"candidates\": [\n        {\"text\": \"## Other refusals and their codes\", \"document\": \"source_a.md\"},\n        {\"text\": \"## Other kinds of refusal\", \"document\": \"source_b.md\"}\n      ],\n      \"chosen\": \"## Other refusals and their codes\",\n      \"reason\": \"Base heading kept for the non-cap refusals section.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"dispositions\": [\n    {\"segment\":\"b1\",\"disposition\":\"superseded\",\"replacement\":\"# Rate limiting and the 429 contract for gateway clients\",\"reason\":\"Title not chosen; base title used instead.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b2\",\"disposition\":\"duplicate\",\"replacement\":\"Every credential has a cap.\",\"reason\":\"Same fact as a2, kept once.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b3\",\"disposition\":\"duplicate\",\"replacement\":\"When that cap is spent the gateway answers 429 at the edge — without waking the service behind it — so the refusal costs the platform almost nothing and costs a caller that files it under transport error a great deal.\",\"reason\":\"Same fact as a3, kept once.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b4\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"An earlier draft of this note argued for a client-side token bucket that mirrored the gateway's own counters; that section was left out on purpose, since two counters that disagree are worse than one counter that refuses, which is the whole reason the header exists.\",\"reason\":\"Rationale for omitting client-side counters carried in.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"a4\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"Requests are counted against the credential that presented them, over a fixed window of 60 seconds whose start the gateway doesn't disclose, and never against a connection.\",\"reason\":\"Window length and undisclosed start combined.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b5\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"Requests are counted against the credential that presented them, over a fixed window of 60 seconds whose start the gateway doesn't disclose, and never against a connection.\",\"reason\":\"Window length and undisclosed start combined.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"a5\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"Opening a second socket therefore buys a caller nothing, since the count follows the credential wherever it goes, including across hosts.\",\"reason\":\"Socket futility combined with cross-host detail.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b6\",\"disposition\":\"duplicate\",\"replacement\":\"Opening a second socket therefore buys a caller nothing, since the count follows the credential wherever it goes, including across hosts.\",\"reason\":\"Same fact as a5.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b7\",\"disposition\":\"subsumed\",\"replacement\":\"Opening a second socket therefore buys a caller nothing, since the count follows the credential wherever it goes, including across hosts.\",\"reason\":\"Cross-host detail folded into a5's statement.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b8\",\"disposition\":\"superseded\",\"replacement\":\"A client spread across eight worker processes is throttled at exactly the point one process would have been — and pays for the extra file descriptors as well.\",\"reason\":\"Duplicate of a6, which keeps the specific figure.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"a7\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"The default allowance is 100 requests in a window — enough for every interactive use of this API the platform team has seen, since a caller that needs more is almost always doing batch work under an interactive credential — and a credential marked for batch work is allowed 1000 requests in the same window.\",\"reason\":\"Allowance figure and its rationale combined.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b9\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"The default allowance is 100 requests in a window — enough for every interactive use of this API the platform team has seen, since a caller that needs more is almost always doing batch work under an interactive credential — and a credential marked for batch work is allowed 1000 requests in the same window.\",\"reason\":\"Allowance figure and its rationale combined.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"a8\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"A refusal is not an outage, whatever the error class in a client library happens to call it, and it isn't a bug in the gateway either — it is capacity being held for a request somebody else has already been promised, and waiting is the only correct answer.\",\"reason\":\"Outage, bug and capacity claims combined.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b10\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"A refusal is not an outage, whatever the error class in a client library happens to call it, and it isn't a bug in the gateway either — it is capacity being held for a request somebody else has already been promised, and waiting is the only correct answer.\",\"reason\":\"Outage, bug and capacity claims combined.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"a9\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"A refusal is not an outage, whatever the error class in a client library happens to call it, and it isn't a bug in the gateway either — it is capacity being held for a request somebody else has already been promised, and waiting is the only correct answer.\",\"reason\":\"Outage, bug and capacity claims combined.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b11\",\"disposition\":\"superseded\",\"replacement\":\"Callers that retry at once are the largest single reason the cap is there at all.\",\"reason\":\"Duplicate of a10.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b12\",\"disposition\":\"duplicate\",\"replacement\":\"## How to read a refusal\",\"reason\":\"Identical heading text to a11.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b13\",\"disposition\":\"superseded\",\"replacement\":\"The refusal carries a Retry-After header.\",\"reason\":\"Duplicate of a12.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"a13\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"Its value is a whole number of seconds to wait, and it is present on every refusal the gateway sends.\",\"reason\":\"Wait duration and universality combined.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b14\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"Its value is a whole number of seconds to wait, and it is present on every refusal the gateway sends.\",\"reason\":\"Wait duration and universality combined.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b15\",\"disposition\":\"superseded\",\"replacement\":\"Waiting that long and then continuing is the whole of the contract — a client that does it needs nothing else from this document.\",\"reason\":\"Same contract stated; a14's wording kept.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b16\",\"disposition\":\"superseded\",\"replacement\":\"The gateway keeps no memory of who backed off politely, so waiting longer than the header asks earns a caller no credit at all.\",\"reason\":\"Same fact; a15's wording kept.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b17\",\"disposition\":\"superseded\",\"replacement\":\"The body of the refusal is JSON, and it names what the gateway calls the \\\"window\\\" it counted in. It names the cap and the tier as well.\",\"reason\":\"Duplicate description of body fields.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b18\",\"disposition\":\"superseded\",\"replacement\":\"None of those fields is a substitute for the header, and a caller that parses the body to compute its own wait is doing the same arithmetic twice.\",\"reason\":\"Same critique; a18's wording kept.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b19\",\"disposition\":\"superseded\",\"replacement\":\"The body is there so that a human reading a log afterwards can see why the request was refused — nothing in it is meant for the retry loop.\",\"reason\":\"a19 states the same fact more completely.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b20\",\"disposition\":\"superseded\",\"replacement\":\"## How to retry well\",\"reason\":\"Heading not chosen; base heading used.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b21\",\"disposition\":\"superseded\",\"replacement\":\"Four rules, given in the order they should be applied.\",\"reason\":\"Duplicate of a21.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b22\",\"disposition\":\"superseded\",\"replacement\":\"1. Honour the header, every time.\",\"reason\":\"Duplicate rule label of a22.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"a23\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"The gateway has already done that arithmetic, with information the caller does not have, and it does the same on the 503 path, even though the cause of the refusal there is an entirely different one.\",\"reason\":\"503-path detail combined with the arithmetic claim.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b23\",\"disposition\":\"superseded\",\"replacement\":\"The gateway has already done that arithmetic, with information the caller does not have, and it does the same on the 503 path, even though the cause of the refusal there is an entirely different one.\",\"reason\":\"Duplicate of a23's core statement.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b24\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"The gateway has already done that arithmetic, with information the caller does not have, and it does the same on the 503 path, even though the cause of the refusal there is an entirely different one.\",\"reason\":\"503-path detail combined with the arithmetic claim.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b25\",\"disposition\":\"superseded\",\"replacement\":\"A wait derived on the client side is a guess about a counter it cannot see.\",\"reason\":\"Duplicate of a24.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b26\",\"disposition\":\"superseded\",\"replacement\":\"There is no case at all where a guess is the better of the two.\",\"reason\":\"Duplicate of a25.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"a26\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"2. Retry only what is safe to retry, which is a smaller set than the set of responses that aren't a success.\",\"reason\":\"Rule text combined with the scope clarification.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b27\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"2. Retry only what is safe to retry, which is a smaller set than the set of responses that aren't a success.\",\"reason\":\"Rule text combined with the scope clarification.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b28\",\"disposition\":\"superseded\",\"replacement\":\"A 200 wants nothing, a 429 wants the stated wait, a 500 may be retried once that wait has passed, and a 503 is the overload path.\",\"reason\":\"Duplicate of a27.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b29\",\"disposition\":\"superseded\",\"replacement\":\"A client that folds every non-success into one branch will retry hardest during exactly the incident the cap was installed to survive.\",\"reason\":\"Duplicate of a29.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b30\",\"disposition\":\"superseded\",\"replacement\":\"Avoid writing that loop.\",\"reason\":\"Same point as a30.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"a32\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"A batch credential is allowed 1000 requests in a window — fifty times the default allowance — measured over exactly the same window, not as a separate counting scheme.\",\"reason\":\"Allowance figure and window-scope wording combined.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b31\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"A batch credential is allowed 1000 requests in a window — fifty times the default allowance — measured over exactly the same window, not as a separate counting scheme.\",\"reason\":\"Allowance figure and window-scope wording combined.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b33\",\"disposition\":\"superseded\",\"replacement\":\"Nothing else about the two tiers differs in any way.\",\"reason\":\"Duplicate of a34.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"a35\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"4. Log every refusal seen, and keep the log for at least a week.\",\"reason\":\"Rule text combined with retention period.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b34\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"4. Log every refusal seen, and keep the log for at least a week.\",\"reason\":\"Rule text combined with retention period.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b35\",\"disposition\":\"superseded\",\"replacement\":\"A caller that cannot say how often it was refused last week cannot argue for a larger cap, and nobody at the far end will assemble that argument on its behalf.\",\"reason\":\"Duplicate of a36.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b37\",\"disposition\":\"superseded\",\"replacement\":\"## Other refusals and their codes\",\"reason\":\"Heading not chosen; base heading used.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"a40\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"The gateway refuses a request for three reasons and only one of them is the one above; two of the three have nothing to do with how much traffic a caller has sent in the window it is currently in.\",\"reason\":\"Reason count combined with irrelevance detail.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b38\",\"disposition\":\"superseded\",\"replacement\":\"The gateway refuses a request for three reasons and only one of them is the one above; two of the three have nothing to do with how much traffic a caller has sent in the window it is currently in.\",\"reason\":\"Duplicate of a40's core statement.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b39\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"The gateway refuses a request for three reasons and only one of them is the one above; two of the three have nothing to do with how much traffic a caller has sent in the window it is currently in.\",\"reason\":\"Reason count combined with irrelevance detail.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"a41\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"A credential may be suspended, a route may be closed for maintenance, or a body may exceed the 5 megabyte limit and be refused before it has been read at all.\",\"reason\":\"Refusal causes combined with read-before-refusal detail.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b40\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"A credential may be suspended, a route may be closed for maintenance, or a body may exceed the 5 megabyte limit and be refused before it has been read at all.\",\"reason\":\"Refusal causes combined with read-before-refusal detail.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"a43\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"The distinction matters more to a retry loop than it does to a reader.\",\"reason\":\"Comparison to a reader added to the base claim.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b41\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"The distinction matters more to a retry loop than it does to a reader.\",\"reason\":\"Comparison to a reader added to the base claim.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"a44\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"A loop that waits and retries against a suspended credential spends its whole budget without ever reaching the service — and the log afterwards shows nothing at all except a long run of refusals, which reads like an outage and isn't one, with the wasted budget being the caller's own.\",\"reason\":\"Budget loss combined with outage-resemblance detail.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b42\",\"disposition\":\"superseded\",\"replacement\":\"A loop that waits and retries against a suspended credential spends its whole budget without ever reaching the service — and the log afterwards shows nothing at all except a long run of refusals, which reads like an outage and isn't one, with the wasted budget being the caller's own.\",\"reason\":\"Duplicate of a44's core statement.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b43\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"A loop that waits and retries against a suspended credential spends its whole budget without ever reaching the service — and the log afterwards shows nothing at all except a long run of refusals, which reads like an outage and isn't one, with the wasted budget being the caller's own.\",\"reason\":\"Budget loss combined with outage-resemblance detail.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b46\",\"disposition\":\"superseded\",\"replacement\":\"A cap can be raised, and the number of caps raised without a measurement behind them is 0.\",\"reason\":\"Same policy; a47's numeric wording kept.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b47\",\"disposition\":\"superseded\",\"replacement\":\"Bring a week of refusal counts, the shape of the traffic across the day, and the deadline that traffic is serving.\",\"reason\":\"Duplicate of a48.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"a49\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"The platform team looks at whether the load is smooth or bursty before it looks at the number at all — a burst is cheaper to smooth than it is to serve at its peak.\",\"reason\":\"Evaluation-order detail combined with base claim.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b48\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"The platform team looks at whether the load is smooth or bursty before it looks at the number at all — a burst is cheaper to smooth than it is to serve at its peak.\",\"reason\":\"Evaluation-order detail combined with base claim.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b49\",\"disposition\":\"superseded\",\"replacement\":\"The platform team looks at whether the load is smooth or bursty before it looks at the number at all — a burst is cheaper to smooth than it is to serve at its peak.\",\"reason\":\"Duplicate of a49's burst clause.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"a50\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"A caller that can move half its work to a quieter hour usually gets what it needs without any change to the cap at all, which is the outcome everyone prefers; it is the cheapest fix available to anybody, and it is available to almost everybody.\",\"reason\":\"Preference combined with cheapest-fix detail.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b44\",\"disposition\":\"superseded\",\"replacement\":\"A caller that can move half its work to a quieter hour usually gets what it needs without any change to the cap at all, which is the outcome everyone prefers; it is the cheapest fix available to anybody, and it is available to almost everybody.\",\"reason\":\"Duplicate of a50's core statement.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b45\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"A caller that can move half its work to a quieter hour usually gets what it needs without any change to the cap at all, which is the outcome everyone prefers; it is the cheapest fix available to anybody, and it is available to almost everybody.\",\"reason\":\"Preference combined with cheapest-fix detail.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"a51\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"Requests reach the platform team through the usual channel, and they are answered within two working days — chasing it doesn't make that take fewer.\",\"reason\":\"Turnaround time combined with chasing detail.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b50\",\"disposition\":\"superseded\",\"replacement\":\"Requests reach the platform team through the usual channel, and they are answered within two working days — chasing it doesn't make that take fewer.\",\"reason\":\"Duplicate of a51's core statement.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b52\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"Requests reach the platform team through the usual channel, and they are answered within two working days — chasing it doesn't make that take fewer.\",\"reason\":\"Turnaround time combined with chasing detail.\"},\n    {\"segment\":\"b51\",\"disposition\":\"superseded\",\"replacement\":\"There is no expedited path and no exception list.\",\"reason\":\"Duplicate of a52.\"}\n  ],\n  \"mismatch\": \"\"\n}\n```","ttft_ms":196314,"type":"result","duration_ms":245145,"uuid":"f87cf783-2344-40b4-8ba8-04a1ec506f60","ttft_stream_ms":1820,"time_to_request_ms":100,"first_content_frame_ms":1821,"queued_tu